  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 233 PM EDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nCurrently, ridging is in place aloft with a closed low ejecting into \nthe southern Rockies. With high pressure remaining in place across \nthe eastern U.S. and southerly flow, dry and warm conditions can be \nexpected with temperatures well above normal. By Thursday, the \nclosed low will eject into the Great Plains, leading to the \ndevelopment of a surface low and open wave / surface frontal \nboundary. Locally, ridging will increase with 500mb heights reaching \nabove 5,880m, values typical in July. This makes the case for \nfurther surface heating and highs reaching well into the 80s to low \n90s in the south. Also, as recent high pressure shifts off to the \neast, increasing moisture can also be expected. \n\nBy Friday, the closed low/shortwave will lift to the northeast with \nthe frontal boundary approaching from the northwest ahead of \nCanadian high pressure. With continued moisture advection, this \npattern will produce widespread rain chances through the weekend as \nthe front stays near or north of the area. PWAT values will reach \nabove 1.6 inches, which is above the 90th percentile, suggesting \nlocally heavy rainfall rates are possible. There are also \nindications of some elevated instability early in the weekend, which \nwould be sufficient for at least low-end chances for convection. \nOverall QPF totals have decreased, but there is still a low \nprobability for hazardous weather for isolated flooding. \n\nEarly next week, a pattern shift is expected as high pressure \nexpands from the north, pushing the front south of our area. This \nwill lead to northerly flow and cooler and drier conditions, \nespecially at night. At this time, models differ on the southward \nextent of this frontal boundary progression, which would affect how \nmuch cooler of conditions are expected.",
